WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:08.330
An ideal quantum computer can break the encryption
standards we use today by finding prime factors

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:13.140
of a large integer in just minutes instead
of the thousands of years it would take for

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.450
a classical computer to do.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.890
But before you start to panic, while we have
real quantum hardware today,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.650
it's not quite powerful enough to do that
just yet.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:27.010
However, technologies are advancing faster
than ever.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:32.140
The cell phones we have today are more powerful
than the mainframes that we used to send people

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.140
to the moon.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:38.770
And the researchers believe that we will soon
be entering an era of quantum advances where

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:45.170
quantum computers will be used to accelerate
classical computers, just like GPUs.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:52.260
In this video, I'm going to talk about five
foundational topics in quantum computers:

00:00:52.260 --> 00:01:00.790
superposition, gates, measurement, interference,
and entanglement.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:05.229
But before we dive into that, let's first talk
about bits.

00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:12.380
Classical computers that use bits which are
like switches that can be a 0 or a 1.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:18.200
This way of computation has served us well.
So well, in fact, that almost all modern computers

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.680
work this way.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:27.090
However, this approach doesn't solve all the
problems that we have today -- problems that

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:32.410
can blow up exponentially and would take a
classical computers decades or more to solve.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:36.970
We already talked about the algorithm we
use for encryption.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:45.290
Other types of difficult problems include
optimization, chemistry simulation, and machine

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:46.490
learning.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:50.150
Now let's talk about our first topic, superposition.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:55.900
A quantum computer does not use the simple 0
and 1 bits.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:00.670
Instead, it uses qubits.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:15.180
Qubits can be a 0, a 1, or any linear combination
of the two.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:22.739
This spectrum of states is what we called
a superposition.

00:02:22.739 --> 00:02:27.010
Our next next topic is about gates.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:34.779
Similar to classical computers, we use -- we
string together qubits using a construct

00:02:34.779 --> 00:02:41.129
called gates that can alter the states of qubits into
circuits.

00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:45.032
For example, we can have a qubit

00:02:45.032 --> 00:02:48.719
that's at the state of 0.

00:02:48.719 --> 00:03:01.299
Then we can use Hadamard gate, or H gate for short,
to put it in a superposition between 0 and 1.

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:14.879
And, of course, you can have multiple qubits
with multiple gates in a circuit.

00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:20.309
For the circuit will be useful, at some point
that you need to read about its outputs.

00:03:20.309 --> 00:03:26.879
Which brings us to our next topic, measurement.

00:03:26.879 --> 00:03:35.780
When a qubit is measured, it loses its superposition
and collapses into just a simple 0 or 1. That means

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.280
an arrow pointing this way does not measure
a 0.5,

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:48.480
instead, it has a 50% chance of measuring
a 0 and 50% chance of measuring a 1.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:55.150
It is this in-between state that sometimes
people say that a qubit can be a 0 and a 1

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:56.579
at the same time.

00:03:56.579 --> 00:04:04.729
It also means that just a small number of
qubits can represent a large amount of information.

00:04:04.729 --> 00:04:09.519
So for our next topic, interference,

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:11.799
we begin by addressing a common problem --

00:04:11.799 --> 00:04:20.380
common question -- why is it that quantum
computers can outperform classical ones?

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:30.700
So, if you remember, a quantum state is
a linear combination of the 0 state

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.880
and the 1 state.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:41.950
So, an operation applied to this can be seen
as applying to the 0 state and the 1 state,

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:45.870
doing two calculations at once.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:51.440
It is this parallel computation that gives
quantum its unique advantage.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:05:00.730
However, as you may recall, when a qubit is
measured, it loses its superposition and collapses

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:03.070
into 0 or 1.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:08.450
That means we can only get a single answer
instead of all the answers from this parallel

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:09.540
computation.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:16.160
And to make sure the single answer we get
is a correct one,

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.430
quantum gates need to be arranged in a way

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:20.980
so that

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:27.730
it would amplify the correct answer and cancel
all the incorrect ones.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:33.020
A process called interference.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:38.520
Now this leads us to our last topic, entanglement.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.540
When qubits are entangled, their states become
strongly correlated.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:44.540
That is, changing

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:48.630
the state of just one qubit would change
the state of another.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:56.520
For example, we can entangle two qubits so
that their states have 50% chance of measuring

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:05.650
a 00 and 50% chance of measuring a 11, but
never a 01 or a 10.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:12.000
In this case, if we just -- if we change just
the state of one, the other one would

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.830
also change.

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:20.640
So with the combined power of superposition,

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:23.960
interference,

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:26.470
and entanglement,

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:31.850
quantum computers can solve things that classical
computers simply cannot do today.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:40.740
It can lead to better drug -- better drug discovery --
or enhance the stock portfolio or even artificial

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.300
intelligence.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:46.110
Now we just need to wait for the quantum hardware
to catch up.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.560
Thanks for watching.

